# 50 hard English passages for Live Typing, 13 November 2026 (evergreen topics). Set 3.
EN = [
("The Finance Commission", "The Finance Commission is a constitutional body set up under Article 280 every five years, or earlier if the President considers it necessary. It consists of a chairman and four other members with expertise in public finance, administration and economics. Its main task is to recommend how the net proceeds of shareable central taxes should be divided between the Union and the States, and how the States' share should be distributed among them. The criteria used for horizontal distribution have included population, area, forest cover, income distance and demographic performance, the last of which rewards States that have controlled population growth. The Commission also recommends grants-in-aid for States that need assistance, as well as grants for rural and urban local bodies and for disaster management. Successive Commissions have steadily increased the States' share in the divisible pool, giving them greater freedom to spend according to their own priorities. However, the growing use of cesses and surcharges by the Union, which are outside the divisible pool, has reduced the effective transfer. Although its recommendations are advisory, they are generally accepted by the government."),
("Coalition Governments", "A coalition government is formed when no single party wins a majority in the legislature and two or more parties agree to share power on the basis of a common programme. India experienced coalition governments at the Centre for long periods from the late nineteen-eighties, and many States have been ruled by alliances of national and regional parties. Supporters argue that coalitions make governments more representative of India's diversity, because regional concerns find a voice at the national level and policies are made through consultation and compromise. Coalitions also discourage authoritarian tendencies, since the leading party must carry its partners along. Critics, on the other hand, point to instability, frequent bargaining over ministerial posts, delays in difficult reforms and the tendency of small partners to exert influence far beyond their strength. To manage these tensions, coalitions have relied on coordination committees, a common minimum programme and regular meetings of alliance leaders. The experience shows that the success of a coalition depends less on numbers than on trust, clear agreements and the willingness of partners to place governance above narrow interests."),
("The GST Council", "The Goods and Services Tax Council was created under Article 279A, inserted by the One Hundred and First Amendment in 2016, as a joint forum of the Union and the States. It is chaired by the Union Finance Minister and includes the Union Minister of State for Finance and the finance ministers of all States and Union Territories with legislatures. The Council recommends tax rates, exemptions, thresholds, model laws and procedures for the tax. Decisions require a majority of not less than three-fourths of the weighted votes of members present, with the Union holding one-third of the weight and the States together holding two-thirds. This structure means that neither the Union nor the States can impose decisions on their own. The Council has largely worked by consensus, rationalising rates, simplifying returns and resolving disputes over compensation. The Supreme Court observed in 2022 that its recommendations have persuasive value rather than binding force, emphasising cooperative federalism. Continuing challenges include the number of rate slabs, the exclusion of petroleum products and electricity, and the need to strengthen the revenues of States after the end of compensation."),
("Foreign Exchange Reserves", "Foreign exchange reserves are assets held by the central bank in foreign currencies, gold, special drawing rights and the reserve position in the International Monetary Fund. In India, these reserves are managed by the Reserve Bank and are invested mainly in safe and liquid instruments such as government securities of advanced economies and deposits with other central banks. Reserves serve several purposes. They allow the country to pay for imports, service external debt and meet sudden outflows of capital. They also give the central bank the ability to intervene in the currency market to smooth excessive volatility in the exchange rate of the rupee. During the balance of payments crisis of 1991, India's reserves had fallen so low that they could cover only a few weeks of imports, and gold had to be pledged abroad. Since then, reserves have grown substantially through capital inflows, remittances and the earnings of the services sector. Holding large reserves has a cost, because they earn low returns, but most economists consider an adequate buffer essential for a large emerging economy exposed to global shocks."),
("The Current Account Deficit", "The current account records a country's transactions with the rest of the world in goods, services, income and transfers. A deficit arises when the value of imports of goods and services, together with payments of interest and profits abroad, exceeds the earnings from exports, remittances and other receipts. India usually runs a current account deficit because it imports large quantities of crude oil, gold, electronics and machinery, while its exports of goods are smaller. This gap is partly offset by strong exports of software and business services and by remittances sent home by Indians working abroad, which are among the highest in the world. A moderate deficit is not harmful if it is financed by stable capital inflows such as foreign direct investment. A large deficit, however, makes the economy dependent on volatile portfolio flows and exposes the rupee to sudden pressure, as happened during the taper tantrum of 2013. Measures to keep the deficit manageable include boosting manufacturing and exports, reducing dependence on imported energy through renewables, encouraging domestic production of electronics and discouraging non-essential imports."),
("Special Economic Zones", "Special Economic Zones are designated areas where businesses enjoy simpler regulations, tax benefits and better infrastructure, with the aim of promoting exports, attracting investment and creating jobs. India was among the first Asian countries to experiment with export processing zones, setting one up at Kandla in 1965. The Special Economic Zones Act of 2005 gave the policy a fresh push by offering income tax holidays, duty-free imports and single-window clearances, which led to a large number of approvals. The experience, however, was mixed. Many zones remained underused, some developers acquired land mainly for real estate, farmers protested against the acquisition of fertile land, and the tax concessions were criticised for causing loss of revenue without matching gains. When the sunset clause for tax benefits approached and global trade rules discouraged export subsidies, interest declined. The government has proposed replacing the existing law with a new framework of development hubs, which would allow units to sell in the domestic market more easily, focus on employment and infrastructure, and link zones with industrial corridors and ports."),
("Land Acquisition and Fair Compensation", "The acquisition of private land by the government for public purposes has long been a contentious issue in India. For more than a century, it was governed by the Land Acquisition Act of 1894, a colonial law that gave the State wide powers and offered landowners compensation often far below the market value. Protests against acquisitions for industrial projects led Parliament to enact the Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act in 2013. The new law provides for compensation of up to four times the market value in rural areas and twice in urban areas, requires the consent of a large majority of affected families for projects involving private companies, and mandates a social impact assessment before acquisition. It also guarantees rehabilitation and resettlement benefits to landless labourers and others who depend on the land for their livelihood. Industry has complained that these requirements make acquisition slow and costly, while farmer groups insist that they are necessary safeguards. Several States have amended the law for their own needs, and land pooling has emerged as an alternative model."),
("Forest Rights of Tribal Communities", "For generations, tribal and other forest-dwelling communities have lived in and depended on forests for food, fuel, fodder, medicine and livelihood. Colonial and later forest laws, however, treated them as encroachers on government land, denying them secure rights over the very resources they had protected. The Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act of 2006 sought to correct this historical injustice. It recognises individual rights over land under cultivation, community rights over minor forest produce, grazing grounds and water bodies, and the right to protect and manage community forest resources. The gram sabha plays a central role in initiating and verifying claims. Implementation has been uneven. A large number of claims have been rejected, often without proper reasons or hearings, and community rights have been recognised far less frequently than individual titles. Conflicts have also arisen with conservation laws in tiger reserves and with diversion of forest land for mining and infrastructure. Experts argue that empowering communities to govern forests can strengthen both livelihoods and conservation."),
("Tiger Conservation in India", "India is home to the largest population of wild tigers in the world. When the count fell to alarming levels in the early nineteen-seventies, the government launched Project Tiger in 1973, beginning with nine reserves. Today the network includes more than fifty tiger reserves spread across the country, managed under the guidance of the National Tiger Conservation Authority, a statutory body created in 2006. Each reserve has a core area kept free of human disturbance and a buffer zone where regulated activities are permitted. Periodic all-India tiger estimation, using camera traps and scientific sampling, has recorded a steady rise in numbers over recent cycles. Protecting tigers means protecting entire landscapes, including the forests, grasslands and rivers on which countless other species depend. Challenges remain serious. Poaching for illegal trade in skins and body parts, loss and fragmentation of habitat due to roads, railways and mines, and conflict with people living near reserves continue to threaten the species. Wildlife corridors connecting reserves, voluntary relocation of villages with fair compensation, and compensation for livestock losses are important elements of a lasting strategy."),
("Coral Reefs Under Threat", "Coral reefs are underwater structures built over thousands of years by tiny animals called coral polyps, which live in partnership with microscopic algae that give them food and their bright colours. Although they cover a very small part of the ocean floor, reefs support a quarter of all marine species, protect coastlines from waves and storms, and sustain fisheries and tourism. In India, major reefs are found in the Gulf of Mannar, the Gulf of Kachchh, Lakshadweep and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands. Rising sea temperatures cause corals to expel their algae and turn white, a process known as bleaching. If high temperatures persist, the corals die. Mass bleaching events have become more frequent with global warming, and ocean acidification caused by the absorption of carbon dioxide weakens their skeletons. Local pressures such as destructive fishing, coral mining, sewage, sediment from construction and careless tourism add to the damage. Scientists are experimenting with coral gardening and transplantation, while marine protected areas restrict harmful activities. Ultimately, the survival of reefs depends on reducing greenhouse gas emissions worldwide."),
("Protecting the Ozone Layer", "The ozone layer, located in the stratosphere about fifteen to thirty-five kilometres above the earth, absorbs most of the harmful ultraviolet radiation from the sun. Without it, the incidence of skin cancer, cataracts and damage to crops and marine life would rise sharply. In the nineteen-seventies and eighties, scientists discovered that chlorofluorocarbons and other chemicals used in refrigerators, air conditioners, aerosol sprays and fire extinguishers were destroying ozone, and a seasonal hole was observed over Antarctica. The international response was remarkably swift. The Vienna Convention of 1985 was followed by the Montreal Protocol of 1987, which set timetables for phasing out ozone-depleting substances, with financial and technical help for developing countries. India joined the protocol and phased out the production and use of chlorofluorocarbons ahead of schedule. The Kigali Amendment of 2016 extended the protocol to hydrofluorocarbons, which do not harm ozone but are powerful greenhouse gases. Scientists expect the ozone layer to recover to earlier levels over the coming decades, making the protocol one of the most successful environmental agreements ever adopted."),
("Stubble Burning and Air Quality", "Every year after the paddy harvest in October and November, farmers in Punjab, Haryana and parts of western Uttar Pradesh set fire to the crop residue left in their fields. The smoke combines with vehicle exhaust, industrial emissions, construction dust and calm winter weather to produce severe smog across the Indo-Gangetic plain, including Delhi. Farmers burn stubble because the window between harvesting paddy and sowing wheat is short, combine harvesters leave tall stalks, and removing residue manually or with machines is costly. The practice has been encouraged indirectly by a law requiring paddy transplanting to be delayed until the monsoon arrives, in order to save groundwater, which squeezes the time available. Governments have provided subsidies for machines such as the happy seeder and super seeder, which sow wheat directly into the residue, and have promoted bio-decomposer sprays that turn stubble into manure. Collecting residue for use in power plants, biogas units and cardboard factories is another option. Experts emphasise that lasting solutions require crop diversification away from paddy and fair support for farmers rather than penalties alone."),
("Nuclear Energy in India", "Nuclear power provides a steady supply of electricity without emitting greenhouse gases during generation, making it an important part of India's plans for clean energy. India's nuclear programme, envisioned by Homi J. Bhabha, follows a three-stage strategy designed around the country's limited uranium and abundant thorium reserves. The first stage uses pressurised heavy water reactors fuelled by natural uranium, the second stage relies on fast breeder reactors that produce more fissile material than they consume, and the third stage aims to use thorium on a large scale. The prototype fast breeder reactor at Kalpakkam is a key step towards the second stage. After the civil nuclear agreement with the United States in 2008 and a waiver from the Nuclear Suppliers Group, India gained access to imported fuel and reactors. Nuclear power, however, still contributes only a small share of total electricity generation. High capital costs, long construction periods, public concerns about safety after the Fukushima accident, issues of liability for suppliers and the disposal of radioactive waste have slowed expansion. Small modular reactors are now being explored as a flexible option."),
("The Legacy of the Green Revolution", "The Green Revolution of the late nineteen-sixties transformed India from a country dependent on food imports into one that produces a surplus of grain. It combined high-yielding varieties of wheat and rice, developed with the help of scientists such as Norman Borlaug and M. S. Swaminathan, with assured irrigation, chemical fertilisers, pesticides, institutional credit and guaranteed procurement prices. Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh, with their canal networks and enterprising farmers, were the first to benefit, and yields rose dramatically within a decade. The achievement saved millions from hunger and laid the foundation of the public distribution system. Over time, however, its limitations became clear. The benefits were concentrated in a few regions and crops, while pulses, oilseeds and millets were neglected. Intensive use of water has depleted groundwater, excessive fertiliser has damaged soil health, and pesticide use has raised health concerns. Many experts now call for an evergreen revolution, a phrase used by Swaminathan, which would raise productivity without ecological harm through diversified cropping, efficient irrigation, integrated nutrient management and stronger support for rain-fed farming."),
("The White Revolution", "Operation Flood, launched by the National Dairy Development Board in 1970 under the leadership of Verghese Kurien, is often called the White Revolution. It sought to link milk producers in villages with consumers in cities through a network of cooperatives modelled on the Anand pattern in Gujarat. Village dairy cooperatives collected milk from small farmers twice a day, paid them according to fat content, and supplied it to district unions that processed and marketed it under common brands. The programme used food aid received as gifts, such as milk powder and butter oil, to raise funds for building infrastructure, so that imported surplus helped create domestic capacity. Within a few decades, India became the largest producer of milk in the world. The dairy sector now provides a regular income to crores of rural households, a large proportion of them landless or marginal farmers and women. Challenges include low productivity of indigenous cattle, shortage of fodder, inadequate veterinary services, adulteration and the need to protect small producers from volatile prices. Breed improvement, better feed and cold chains are priorities."),
("The Food Processing Industry", "Food processing transforms raw agricultural produce into products with a longer shelf life and higher value, such as packaged milk, juices, pickles, flour, frozen vegetables and ready-to-eat meals. India is one of the largest producers of fruits, vegetables, milk and grains in the world, yet only a small proportion of its farm output is processed, and a significant share of perishable produce is lost after harvest because of poor storage and transport. A strong processing industry can reduce this wastage, increase farmers' incomes, generate employment in rural areas and boost exports. The Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana supports mega food parks, cold chains and agro-processing clusters, while a production-linked incentive scheme rewards companies that increase sales of processed foods. The PM Formalisation of Micro Food Processing Enterprises scheme helps small units with credit, branding and training. Obstacles include fragmented supply chains, high costs of packaging, multiple regulations and inconsistent quality standards. Linking farmer producer organisations directly with processors and investing in testing laboratories and warehouses near farms are considered essential for the sector's growth."),
("Teacher Education and the Quality of Schooling", "No education system can rise above the quality of its teachers. Studies of learning levels in India have repeatedly shown that many children in the middle and upper primary classes cannot read simple texts or solve basic arithmetic problems suited to their grade, even though enrolment is nearly universal. A large part of the explanation lies in teacher preparation and support. For years, a large number of teacher training institutions offered poor-quality courses with little practical exposure, and some functioned merely as degree-selling shops. Teacher vacancies, single-teacher schools in remote areas and the burden of non-teaching duties such as surveys and election work further reduce classroom time. The National Education Policy of 2020 proposes a four-year integrated Bachelor of Education programme, stricter regulation of training institutions, continuous professional development of fifty hours a year, and merit-based recruitment and promotion. It also stresses foundational literacy and numeracy in the early years. Experts add that teachers need respect, reasonable workloads, mentoring and freedom to adapt teaching to the needs of their pupils."),
("Regulating Higher Education", "India has one of the largest higher education systems in the world, with more than a thousand universities and tens of thousands of colleges. It is regulated by several bodies, including the University Grants Commission, the All India Council for Technical Education and the National Council for Teacher Education, along with separate councils for medicine, law and architecture. Critics have long argued that this structure is fragmented, focused on inspections and paperwork rather than outcomes, and prone to delays and corruption. The National Education Policy of 2020 proposes a single umbrella regulator, the Higher Education Commission of India, with separate verticals for regulation, accreditation, funding and setting academic standards. It also aims to raise the gross enrolment ratio substantially, allow multiple entry and exit options, introduce an academic bank of credits and encourage multidisciplinary universities. Graded autonomy would allow well-performing institutions to design courses and manage their affairs with less interference. Experts caution that reforms must be accompanied by adequate public funding, recruitment of qualified faculty, investment in research and safeguards to keep higher education affordable."),
("Eliminating Tuberculosis", "Tuberculosis is an infectious disease caused by bacteria that most often attack the lungs and spread through the air when an infected person coughs or sneezes. India bears the largest share of the global burden of the disease, and it remains one of the leading causes of death among adults. Poverty, undernutrition, overcrowded housing, diabetes, smoking and HIV infection increase the risk. The National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme provides free diagnosis and treatment through public health facilities, including rapid molecular tests that can detect the disease and drug resistance within hours. Patients receive financial support for nutrition through direct benefit transfer under the Nikshay Poshan Yojana, and the Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan invites individuals and organisations to adopt patients and provide food baskets. Drug-resistant tuberculosis, which requires longer and more toxic treatment, is a serious challenge. Many patients first visit private practitioners, so notification and quality of care in the private sector are crucial. Stigma, delays in seeking care and treatment interruptions must be overcome through community support and counselling."),
("Mental Health Care in India", "Mental health conditions such as depression, anxiety, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia and substance use disorders affect a large number of people in India, yet most of them receive no treatment. The treatment gap arises from a severe shortage of psychiatrists, psychologists and psychiatric nurses, especially outside cities, from low public spending, and from stigma that prevents people from seeking help. The Mental Healthcare Act of 2017 marked a major shift towards a rights-based approach. It guarantees every person the right to access mental health care and treatment from services run or funded by the government, allows individuals to make advance directives about their treatment, and restricts practices such as chaining and electroconvulsive therapy without anaesthesia. The Act also effectively decriminalised attempted suicide by presuming that a person who attempts it is under severe stress. The District Mental Health Programme and the Tele-MANAS helpline extend services to communities. Experts stress the importance of integrating mental health into primary care, training general doctors and community workers, promoting well-being in schools and workplaces, and treating mental illness with the same seriousness as physical illness."),
("Organ Donation", "Organ donation allows a person to give organs such as kidneys, liver, heart, lungs and pancreas, or tissues such as corneas and skin, to save or improve the lives of others. Every year, thousands of patients in India die waiting for a transplant because the number of available organs falls far short of the need. Donation can be made by a living donor, usually a close relative, or by a deceased donor after brain death has been certified by a board of doctors. The Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act regulates the process, prohibits commercial dealings in organs and lays down strict procedures for authorisation to prevent exploitation of the poor. The National Organ and Tissue Transplant Organisation maintains registries and coordinates allocation through regional and State networks. Some States, particularly Tamil Nadu, have built effective systems for deceased donation through trained coordinators in hospitals. Major barriers include lack of awareness, myths and religious misconceptions, reluctance of grieving families, and inadequate facilities for maintaining brain-dead donors. Pledging organs and discussing the decision with family members can make a vital difference."),
("Inland Waterways", "Rivers and canals offer a cheap, fuel-efficient and environment-friendly way of moving bulk cargo such as coal, cement, fertilisers and food grains. Moving goods by water consumes much less fuel per tonne-kilometre than road or rail transport, reduces congestion on highways and lowers emissions. Despite having an extensive network of navigable rivers, India has historically used waterways for only a tiny share of its freight. The National Waterways Act of 2016 declared more than a hundred rivers and canals as national waterways, adding to the five already recognised. The most developed is National Waterway One on the Ganga between Haldia and Prayagraj, where the Jal Marg Vikas Project has built multimodal terminals at Varanasi, Sahibganj and Haldia and a navigation lock at Farakka. The Brahmaputra and the Barak in the north-east also carry cargo, including trade with Bangladesh under a bilateral protocol. Obstacles include seasonal variations in water depth, siltation, the need for constant dredging, a shortage of suitable vessels and poor connections with roads and railways. Careful attention to river ecology is also necessary."),
("High-Speed Rail", "High-speed trains, which run at speeds above two hundred and fifty kilometres per hour on dedicated tracks, have transformed travel in countries such as Japan, France and China by connecting major cities in a fraction of the time taken by conventional trains. India's first high-speed rail corridor is being built between Mumbai and Ahmedabad, covering about five hundred and eight kilometres, with technical and financial assistance from Japan based on the Shinkansen system. Most of the route is elevated, and it includes an undersea tunnel near Mumbai. When completed, the journey is expected to take around two to three hours, compared with six to eight hours by existing trains. Supporters argue that high-speed rail will promote economic integration, reduce air and road traffic between the two cities, create skilled jobs and transfer advanced technology to Indian industry. Critics question the high cost, the burden of foreign loans, and whether the money could have been better spent upgrading the existing network for millions of ordinary passengers. Delays in land acquisition in the early years pushed back the timeline of the project."),
("Regional Air Connectivity", "For many decades, air travel in India remained largely confined to metropolitan cities, while smaller towns lacked airports or regular flights. The Regional Connectivity Scheme, known as UDAN, an abbreviation of a Hindi phrase meaning that the common citizen should fly, was launched in 2016 to make flying affordable and to connect underserved airports. Under the scheme, airlines bid for routes and receive financial support through viability gap funding, while the Centre, States and airport operators offer concessions such as reduced charges and taxes. In return, airlines cap fares on a portion of their seats. Hundreds of routes have been started, reviving unused airstrips and adding helicopter and seaplane services in hilly and island regions. The scheme has improved access to remote areas, encouraged tourism and supported local economies. However, a number of routes were discontinued after the subsidy period ended because of low demand, unsuitable aircraft or operational difficulties faced by smaller airlines. Sustainable regional aviation requires realistic route planning, reliable maintenance facilities, trained pilots and integration with the tourism and trade potential of each region."),
("Rural Road Connectivity", "Roads are the lifeline of rural India, connecting villages to markets, schools, hospitals and government offices. Before the turn of the century, a large number of habitations had no all-weather road, leaving them cut off during the monsoon. The Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana, launched in December 2000, aimed to provide all-weather road connectivity to eligible unconnected habitations above a certain population size, with lower thresholds for hill, desert and tribal areas. The scheme was fully funded by the Centre in its early phases and is now shared with the States. It introduced standards for road design, quality monitoring by independent inspectors and an online management system to track progress. Later phases focused on upgrading existing routes and on roads in areas affected by left-wing extremism. Studies have found that rural roads raise agricultural incomes by reducing transport costs, increase school attendance, especially among girls, and improve access to health care. Maintenance remains the weakest link, because many roads deteriorate within a few years when States do not allocate sufficient funds after the construction contract ends."),
("Housing for All", "Adequate housing is a basic human need, yet millions of families in India live in kutcha houses, overcrowded tenements or urban slums without secure tenure, sanitation or clean water. The Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana, launched in 2015 with urban and rural components, aims to provide pucca houses with basic amenities to eligible families. In rural areas, beneficiaries selected on the basis of socio-economic census data receive financial assistance in instalments linked to the progress of construction, with additional support for toilets and wages under the rural employment guarantee scheme. In cities, the scheme offers interest subsidies on home loans, assistance for houses built by beneficiaries themselves, partnerships for affordable housing projects and slum redevelopment. Houses are often registered in the name of a woman or jointly, strengthening women's ownership of assets. Challenges include the availability of land in cities, cost escalation, delays in releasing instalments and the poor location of some projects far from places of work. Rental housing for migrants, better quality construction and access to transport are considered essential complements to home ownership."),
("Solid Waste Management in Cities", "Indian cities generate huge quantities of solid waste every day, and the amount is rising with urbanisation and changing patterns of consumption. For years, most of it was dumped in open landfills on the edges of cities, where it polluted groundwater, released methane and caught fire, while waste pickers worked in hazardous conditions. The Solid Waste Management Rules of 2016 require every household and establishment to segregate waste at source into wet, dry and hazardous categories, and make urban local bodies responsible for door-to-door collection, processing and scientific disposal. Bulk generators such as hotels and housing societies must process their own wet waste. Under the Swachh Bharat Mission, cities have been ranked through annual cleanliness surveys, and Indore has repeatedly been placed at the top for its systems of segregation and composting. Wet waste can be converted into compost or biogas, while dry waste can be recycled. Legacy waste in old dumpsites is being cleared through biomining. Success depends on citizen participation, recognition of informal waste pickers, adequate funding and reducing the use of single-use plastic."),
("Tap Water for Every Rural Home", "Access to safe drinking water at home saves women and girls countless hours spent fetching water from distant sources and protects families from waterborne diseases. Until recently, only a small fraction of rural households in India had a tap connection. The Jal Jeevan Mission, launched in 2019, set the goal of providing functional household tap connections with water of prescribed quality and adequate quantity to every rural home. The mission follows a decentralised approach in which village water and sanitation committees, with at least half women members, plan, implement and maintain the schemes, and communities contribute a small share of the capital cost. Field testing kits have been distributed so that trained local women can test water quality regularly. The number of rural households with tap connections has increased dramatically since the mission began. Ensuring sustainability, however, remains a major challenge. Taps are of little use if sources dry up in summer, so source strengthening through groundwater recharge, rainwater harvesting and the reuse of grey water is essential. Regular maintenance, fair tariffs and reliable electricity for pumps are equally important."),
("Delimitation of Constituencies", "Delimitation is the process of fixing the number of seats and redrawing the boundaries of parliamentary and assembly constituencies to reflect changes in population. It is carried out by a Delimitation Commission, a high-powered body set up under a law of Parliament, whose orders have the force of law and cannot be questioned in any court. The Commission also determines the seats to be reserved for the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes. Delimitation was carried out after the Censuses of 1951, 1961 and 1971. In 1976, the number of seats allotted to States was frozen until after the first Census taken after 2000, so that States which had successfully controlled population growth would not lose representation. This freeze was later extended until after the first Census taken after 2026, although the boundaries of constituencies within States were redrawn on the basis of the 2001 Census. The next exercise is therefore politically sensitive. Southern States fear a reduction in their relative share of seats, while others argue for equal value of votes. Any solution will require careful balancing of democratic equality and federal fairness."),
("The Role of the Rajya Sabha", "The Rajya Sabha, or Council of States, is the upper house of Parliament, representing the States and Union Territories. Its members are elected by the elected members of State legislative assemblies through proportional representation by means of the single transferable vote, while twelve members are nominated by the President for their expertise in literature, science, art and social service. It is a permanent body that is never dissolved, with one-third of its members retiring every second year. The Vice-President is its ex officio chairman. The Rajya Sabha shares legislative powers with the Lok Sabha on ordinary bills, but its powers over money bills are limited to making recommendations within fourteen days. It has special powers to authorise Parliament to legislate on a subject in the State List in the national interest and to create new all-India services. Supporters see it as a chamber of sober second thought that scrutinises hasty legislation and gives voice to the States. Critics point to frequent disruptions, the dilution of the domicile requirement for members, and the use of money bills to bypass it."),
("Parliamentary Committees", "Much of the detailed work of Parliament is done not on the floor of the House but in its committees. Because the Houses meet for limited periods and must deal with a large volume of business, committees allow smaller groups of members to examine bills, budgets and the working of ministries in depth, take evidence from officials and experts, and reach conclusions through discussion rather than confrontation. Financial committees include the Public Accounts Committee, traditionally chaired by a member of the opposition, which examines the reports of the Comptroller and Auditor General, the Estimates Committee and the Committee on Public Undertakings. Department-related standing committees, introduced in 1993, scrutinise the demands for grants and bills of the ministries assigned to them. Select and joint committees may be formed to examine particular bills. Committee meetings are held in private, so members can rise above party lines. Observers have expressed concern that a declining share of bills is being referred to committees, reducing scrutiny of important legislation, and that the recommendations of committees are not always acted upon by the government."),
("Question Hour and Zero Hour", "Question Hour, usually the first hour of a sitting of each House of Parliament, is one of the most important instruments for holding the government accountable. Members ask questions about the working of ministries, and ministers must reply. Starred questions require an oral answer and allow supplementary questions, unstarred questions are answered in writing, and short notice questions may be asked on matters of urgent public importance. The answers place information on record, expose shortcomings and often prompt corrective action. Zero Hour is an Indian innovation that has no mention in the rules of procedure. It begins immediately after Question Hour, around noon, when members raise matters of public concern with the permission of the presiding officer, without the usual advance notice. Over the years, frequent disruptions have caused the loss of a large part of Question Hour, and the number of questions answered orally has declined. Many scholars and former presiding officers have argued that Question Hour should be protected from adjournments, that ministers should prepare thoroughly, and that discussion rather than disruption should be the means of raising grievances."),
("The Ordinance-Making Power", "Article 123 of the Constitution empowers the President to promulgate ordinances when either House of Parliament is not in session and circumstances exist that require immediate action. Article 213 gives a similar power to the Governor of a State. An ordinance has the same force as an Act of Parliament, but it must be laid before both Houses when they reassemble and ceases to operate six weeks after that unless approved. The power is exercised on the advice of the Council of Ministers and is meant for genuine emergencies, not as a substitute for legislation. Repeated re-promulgation of ordinances without placing them before the legislature has attracted strong criticism. In D. C. Wadhwa v. State of Bihar (1987), the Supreme Court condemned the practice of re-promulgating ordinances in Bihar many times over, calling it a fraud on the Constitution. In Krishna Kumar Singh v. State of Bihar (2017), a seven-judge Bench held that re-promulgation is unconstitutional and that the satisfaction of the President or Governor is subject to judicial review. These judgments reaffirmed that law-making belongs primarily to the legislature."),
("The Pardoning Power", "Article 72 of the Constitution empowers the President to grant pardons, reprieves, respites and remissions of punishment, or to suspend, remit or commute the sentence of any person convicted of an offence against a Union law, by a court martial, or where the sentence is one of death. Article 161 gives the Governor similar powers for offences against State laws, though the Governor cannot pardon a death sentence. A pardon removes both the sentence and the conviction, commutation substitutes a lighter form of punishment, remission reduces the period of a sentence without changing its character, respite awards a lesser sentence for special reasons such as pregnancy, and reprieve stays the execution of a sentence temporarily. The President exercises this power on the advice of the Council of Ministers. In cases such as Kehar Singh and Epuru Sudhakar, the Supreme Court held that the exercise of this power is subject to limited judicial review on grounds such as mala fides, arbitrariness and the failure to consider relevant material. Unexplained delay in deciding mercy petitions has also been held a ground for commuting a death sentence."),
("The Attorney General of India", "The Attorney General of India is the highest law officer of the country, appointed by the President under Article 76 of the Constitution. A person must be qualified to be appointed a judge of the Supreme Court to hold the office, and serves during the pleasure of the President. The Attorney General gives advice to the Government of India on legal matters referred by the President and performs other duties of a legal character assigned from time to time. The Attorney General represents the government in cases before the Supreme Court and in references made by the President under Article 143, and may appear in any High Court in cases concerning the government. Unusually, the Attorney General has the right to speak and take part in the proceedings of both Houses of Parliament and their committees, though without the right to vote. The office is assisted by the Solicitor General and Additional Solicitors General. Since the Attorney General is not a full-time government servant, private practice is permitted, but not against the government. Questions have occasionally arisen about potential conflicts of interest in politically sensitive cases."),
("The Union Public Service Commission", "The Union Public Service Commission is a constitutional body established under Article 315 to recruit officers for the all-India services and the central civil services through a merit-based and impartial process. Its origins lie in the Public Service Commission set up in 1926 on the recommendation of the Lee Commission. The chairman and members are appointed by the President for a term of six years or until they attain the age of sixty-five years, whichever is earlier, and can be removed only in the manner provided in the Constitution, which protects their independence. Besides conducting examinations such as the civil services examination, the Commission advises the government on methods of recruitment, promotions, transfers and disciplinary matters affecting civil servants. Its advice is not binding, but the government must place an annual report before Parliament explaining cases where the advice was not accepted. The Commission is widely respected for the integrity of its examinations. Debates continue about the length of the recruitment cycle, the pattern of examinations, the balance between generalists and specialists, and the provision for lateral entry of experts into senior positions."),
("The Central Vigilance Commission", "The Central Vigilance Commission was set up in 1964 on the recommendation of the Santhanam Committee on Prevention of Corruption, and was given statutory status by the Central Vigilance Commission Act of 2003, following the Supreme Court's judgment in the Vineet Narain case. It consists of the Central Vigilance Commissioner and up to two Vigilance Commissioners, appointed by the President on the recommendation of a committee headed by the Prime Minister. The Commission supervises vigilance administration in central government ministries, public sector undertakings and public sector banks, and exercises superintendence over the investigation of offences under the Prevention of Corruption Act by the Central Bureau of Investigation. It also advises on disciplinary proceedings against officials and receives complaints from whistle-blowers under the Public Interest Disclosure Resolution. Every organisation has a chief vigilance officer who acts as a link with the Commission. Critics note that the Commission's advice is not binding, that it lacks its own investigating machinery and that it depends on the government for staff. Preventive vigilance through transparency, e-procurement and simplified procedures is increasingly emphasised."),
("The National Human Rights Commission", "The National Human Rights Commission was established in 1993 under the Protection of Human Rights Act as an independent statutory body to protect and promote human rights, defined as the rights relating to life, liberty, equality and dignity guaranteed by the Constitution or embodied in international covenants and enforceable by Indian courts. It is headed by a former Chief Justice or judge of the Supreme Court and includes members with knowledge of human rights, along with the chairpersons of several national commissions as ex officio members. The Commission inquires into complaints of violations or negligence by public servants, either on a petition or on its own motion, visits jails and other institutions, reviews safeguards, and promotes research and awareness. It has issued guidelines on custodial deaths, encounter killings and bonded labour. However, its recommendations are not binding, it cannot investigate complaints more than a year old, and its jurisdiction over the armed forces is limited. Critics also point to the small size of its investigative staff and the large backlog of cases. State Human Rights Commissions perform similar functions at the State level."),
("The Competition Commission of India", "Competition among businesses lowers prices, improves quality and encourages innovation, benefiting consumers and the economy as a whole. The Competition Act of 2002 replaced the Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices Act and established the Competition Commission of India to prevent practices that have an appreciable adverse effect on competition. The Act prohibits anti-competitive agreements, including cartels that fix prices, limit production or rig bids, and abuse of a dominant position, such as predatory pricing or denying market access to rivals. It also requires prior approval for mergers and acquisitions above specified thresholds, so that the Commission can examine whether they would harm competition. The Commission has imposed heavy penalties in cases involving cement companies, airlines, and digital platforms accused of using their dominance in online search, mobile operating systems and e-commerce. Amendments in 2023 introduced a deal value threshold for mergers, settlements and commitments to speed up cases. Digital markets pose new challenges because network effects and control of data can entrench dominance quickly, and a separate law for large digital enterprises has been under discussion."),
("Regulating the Securities Market", "The securities market, where shares, bonds and other financial instruments are bought and sold, channels household savings into productive investment and allows companies to raise capital. Its smooth functioning depends on trust that prices are fair, information is accurate and intermediaries are honest. The Securities and Exchange Board of India, set up as a statutory regulator in 1992 after a major securities scam, protects investors and regulates stock exchanges, brokers, mutual funds, portfolio managers and credit rating agencies. It frames rules on the disclosure of information by listed companies, prohibits insider trading and market manipulation, and oversees public issues of shares. Indian markets have adopted advanced practices such as electronic trading, dematerialised holdings and shortened settlement cycles, which have reduced costs and risks. The number of retail investors has grown rapidly, especially through mutual funds and systematic investment plans. At the same time, the surge in speculative trading in futures and options, in which most individual traders lose money, has prompted the regulator to tighten norms. Financial literacy and vigilance against unregistered advisers on social media remain essential."),
("Regulating Cryptocurrencies", "Cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin are digital assets that use cryptography and decentralised networks called blockchains to record transactions without a central bank or intermediary. Their supporters see them as an innovation that can reduce the cost of payments and promote financial freedom, while critics point to extreme price volatility, their use in fraud, money laundering and ransomware, the absence of any underlying value, and risks to financial stability. The Reserve Bank of India has repeatedly warned the public about these risks. Its 2018 circular barring banks from dealing with crypto businesses was struck down by the Supreme Court in 2020 on the ground of proportionality. The government has since chosen to tax rather than ban them, imposing a flat tax of thirty per cent on gains from virtual digital assets and a one per cent tax deducted at source on transfers, while bringing exchanges under anti-money laundering rules. A comprehensive law has not yet been enacted. Meanwhile, the Reserve Bank has introduced a central bank digital currency, the e-rupee, which uses digital technology while remaining a liability of the central bank."),
("Artificial Intelligence and Employment", "Artificial intelligence refers to computer systems that can perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence, such as recognising speech, translating languages, analysing images, writing text and making predictions from data. Recent advances in machine learning have made such systems far more capable and widely available, raising both hopes and fears about their effect on jobs. Optimists argue that artificial intelligence will boost productivity, create new industries and free workers from routine tasks, as earlier technologies did. Pessimists warn that it may replace large numbers of clerical, customer service and even professional jobs faster than new ones can be created. For India, with its large young workforce and a services sector built on information technology and business process outsourcing, the stakes are high. The government has launched the IndiaAI Mission to build computing infrastructure, develop datasets and models suited to Indian languages, and support startups. Experts emphasise reskilling programmes, the integration of digital and analytical skills into education, social security for displaced workers, and rules to ensure that algorithms used in hiring, lending and policing are fair and transparent."),
("India's Lunar Missions", "India's exploration of the Moon began with Chandrayaan-1, launched by the Indian Space Research Organisation in October 2008. The orbiter carried instruments from several countries and played a key role in detecting evidence of water molecules on the lunar surface, a finding of great scientific importance. Chandrayaan-2, launched in 2019, placed an orbiter around the Moon that continues to send valuable data, but its lander, Vikram, crashed during the final stage of its descent. Learning from that setback, the scientists redesigned the lander with stronger legs, more sensors and greater fuel reserves. On 23 August 2023, Chandrayaan-3 made a successful soft landing near the lunar south pole, making India the first country to land in that region and the fourth to achieve a soft landing on the Moon. The rover Pragyan travelled across the surface and confirmed the presence of sulphur and other elements. The government later declared 23 August as National Space Day. Future plans include a sample return mission and cooperation with Japan on a lunar polar exploration mission, alongside the human spaceflight programme Gaganyaan."),
("Forecasting the Monsoon", "The south-west monsoon brings most of India's annual rainfall between June and September, and its timing, quantity and distribution influence agriculture, water supply, hydropower, rural incomes and inflation. Forecasting it accurately is therefore of national importance. The India Meteorological Department issues a long-range forecast in April, updated in late May or June, predicting whether seasonal rainfall will be deficient, below normal, normal, above normal or excessive compared with the long period average. Earlier statistical models relied on relationships between rainfall and predictors such as sea surface temperatures and snow cover. Since 2021, the department has used a multi-model ensemble that combines statistical methods with dynamic climate models run on high-performance computers. Phenomena such as El Nino in the Pacific Ocean and the Indian Ocean Dipole strongly influence the monsoon, and their evolution is closely monitored. Forecasts at extended range, district level and block level help farmers decide when to sow and which crops to choose. Despite improvements, predicting heavy rainfall events and dry spells within the season remains difficult, especially as climate change increases variability."),
("Cyclone Early Warning", "The Bay of Bengal and the Arabian Sea frequently give rise to tropical cyclones that bring destructive winds, torrential rain and storm surges to India's coasts. In the past, cyclones caused enormous loss of life. The Odisha super cyclone of 1999 killed about ten thousand people. Since then, India has made remarkable progress in reducing deaths through better forecasting and preparedness. The India Meteorological Department, which serves as a regional specialised centre for the North Indian Ocean, uses satellites, Doppler weather radars, ocean buoys and numerical models to track cyclones and predict their path and intensity several days in advance. Warnings are issued in stages, from pre-cyclone watch to post-landfall outlook, and are disseminated through television, radio, mobile messages and local officials. States such as Odisha and Andhra Pradesh have built multi-purpose cyclone shelters, trained volunteers and conducted mock drills, enabling the evacuation of lakhs of people within hours before cyclones such as Phailin in 2013 and Fani in 2019. The focus is now shifting to reducing economic losses through resilient housing, power lines and mangrove belts."),
("Landslides in the Himalaya", "The Himalaya is a young and geologically active mountain range, with steep slopes, fractured rocks and heavy seasonal rainfall that make it highly prone to landslides. Every monsoon, landslides block highways, destroy homes and claim lives in States such as Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh, Sikkim and the north-eastern hills, as well as in the Western Ghats. Human activity has increased the risk. Roads cut into hillsides without adequate drainage and slope protection, unregulated construction of hotels and houses on unstable slopes, deforestation, tunnelling and the dumping of excavated debris into valleys all weaken the terrain. The subsidence of the town of Joshimath in 2023, when cracks appeared in hundreds of buildings, highlighted the dangers of unplanned growth in fragile areas. The Geological Survey of India has prepared landslide susceptibility maps and is developing early warning systems based on rainfall thresholds and ground sensors. Experts recommend strict enforcement of building regulations, carrying capacity studies for hill towns, scientific design of roads, afforestation of slopes, and the active involvement of local communities in monitoring cracks and seepage."),
("Remittances and the Indian Diaspora", "The Indian diaspora, numbering in crores across the world, is one of the largest and most widespread in history. Indians work as labourers, nurses and drivers in the Gulf countries, as engineers, doctors and entrepreneurs in North America, Europe and Australia, and descendants of earlier migrants live in places such as Mauritius, Fiji and the Caribbean. Remittances, the money that migrants send home to their families, make India the largest recipient in the world. These funds support household consumption, education, health care and the construction of houses, especially in States such as Kerala, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar and Punjab, and they help offset India's trade deficit. Unlike foreign investment, remittances tend to remain stable or even rise during crises at home. The diaspora also contributes through investment, philanthropy, transfer of skills and knowledge, and by strengthening India's relations with host countries. Challenges include the exploitation of low-skilled workers by recruitment agents, poor working conditions in some countries, high costs of sending money and difficulties faced by returning migrants in finding work. Better pre-departure training and welfare support can protect workers."),
("The Importance of the Census", "The Census is the most comprehensive source of information about the people of a country. In India, the first synchronous Census was conducted in 1881, and it has been held every ten years since then under the Census Act of 1948, until the exercise due in 2021 was postponed. The Census collects data on population, age, sex, literacy, religion, language, occupation, migration, housing conditions, amenities and disability for every village and town. Its figures are used to delimit constituencies, determine reservation of seats for the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, allocate funds among States and local bodies, and plan schools, hospitals, roads and welfare schemes. Researchers, businesses and planners depend on it for reliable estimates at small geographical levels that sample surveys cannot provide. The Census is conducted by the Office of the Registrar General and Census Commissioner, which also maintains the civil registration system for births and deaths. The forthcoming Census is expected to use digital methods, including mobile applications for enumerators and the option of self-enumeration, which should speed up the release of results."),
('Ethanol Blending and Biofuels', "Biofuels are fuels made from biological materials such as sugarcane, grains, crop residue and used cooking oil. Ethanol, an alcohol produced mainly from sugarcane juice, molasses and surplus grains, can be blended with petrol to reduce the import of crude oil, lower emissions of carbon monoxide and provide farmers with an additional market. India's National Policy on Biofuels, first adopted in 2009 and revised in 2018, set ambitious targets for blending, and the deadline for twenty per cent ethanol blending in petrol was advanced to 2025. Public sector oil companies offer assured prices and long-term contracts to distilleries, and interest subsidies have encouraged new capacity. Second-generation ethanol plants, such as the one at Panipat, convert rice straw and other agricultural waste into fuel, which could also reduce stubble burning. Compressed biogas from organic waste is being promoted under the SATAT initiative. Concerns remain about the large amount of water needed to grow sugarcane, the diversion of food grains to fuel, and the effect of higher blends on the engines of older vehicles. Balancing energy security, food security and water conservation is therefore essential."),
('Self-Rule in Scheduled Areas', "The Panchayats (Extension to the Scheduled Areas) Act of 1996, commonly known as PESA, extended the provisions of the Seventy-third Amendment to the tribal areas listed in the Fifth Schedule of the Constitution, with important modifications to respect their customs and traditions. Based on the recommendations of the Bhuria Committee, the law recognises the gram sabha as the foundation of self-governance. It empowers the gram sabha to safeguard the traditions, cultural identity and community resources of the people, approve development plans, identify beneficiaries of welfare schemes, and be consulted before land is acquired for development projects. It also gives panchayats ownership of minor forest produce, control over local markets and moneylending, and the power to prevent the alienation of tribal land. Ten States have Fifth Schedule areas, but several were slow to frame the rules needed to implement the Act, and in many places the gram sabha's role has remained limited to formal approval. Experts argue that genuine implementation of PESA, together with the Forest Rights Act, can reduce alienation, strengthen democracy at the grassroots and protect the livelihoods of tribal communities."),
]
